			acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'Because Bluetooth audio is compressed, buffered, and transmitted in packets, a delay is built into the pipeline. Video apps compensate automatically by delaying the picture to match the audio clock; games and live monitoring do not, which is why the same headphones feel fine for a film and laggy in a game. Some latency is physics of the standard, not a fault you can fix.' },
		},
		{
			'@type': 'Question',
			name: 'Does a better codec really reduce delay?',
			acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'It can, modestly. A codec is the compression scheme used between the transmitter and headphones, and both ends must support the same one for it to engage. Codecs that send less data per frame or negotiate lower buffer sizes can shave latency, but the difference is usually tens of milliseconds — noticeable against lip-sync failures, not a cure for game lag. No consumer Bluetooth codec eliminates delay.' },
		},
		{
			'@type': 'Question',
			name: 'Why do my headphones sound worse after a phone call?',
			acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'During calls the headphones switch from the high-quality stereo profile to the narrowband hands-free profile used for microphones, and some devices fail to switch back cleanly. Toggle the headphones off and on, or disconnect and reconnect them, to return the link to the stereo audio profile. On Windows you can also disable the Hands-Free Telephony service on the device to stop it being used for listening.' },
		},
		{
			'@type': 'Question',
			name: 'Is wired always better for gaming?',
			acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: 'For competitive play, effectively yes. A wired connection or a dedicated 2.4 GHz wireless gaming dongle adds a few milliseconds, while Bluetooth adds enough that footsteps and shot sounds arrive visibly late. Casual single-player gaming over Bluetooth is perfectly usable; anything where reaction timing is scored is better served by a non-Bluetooth connection.' },
		},
	],
};
